"Steve Lefevre" <lefevre.10@osu.edu> writes:

> Everytime someone tosses out YANI, there is often the complaint that it will
> break save-file format.
> (How much of a problem is this anyway? Do people trade around their games?
> Play the same file on different systems?)

For save files, it's not so much about trading games. 
>From a personal point of view, it's basically about not applying the patch
before all the games going on in that machine are finished. Not a big
issue when there's only one person playing on that machine, but on
multiuser systems this is not always so trivial. Platform-independent
files would also allow playing the same game on different machines..
BONES files are another issue, and mostly their format changes whenever
the save file format does. Many people don't want to lose all their bones
files because of applying a patch.

> I propose that a new save-file format be developed. I'm thinking XML, where
> the program can just ignore data that it doesn't understand.

XML is one possibility, but however it will be done, it will increase the 
file sizes (and break compatibility:-). 
Probably the best non-devteam way implementation would be not to change
the way the game itself handles the files, but make converters that
export the binary files to XML (or some other plain-text format) on one
machine and convert them back to binary format in the other one. These
converters would be compiled on the same machine as the game itself, using
the configuration files and the files generated by makedefs to figure out
the numeric values of all the stuff.
This is not a NI, some people have been talking about such a project, I
remember discussing it with Topi sometimes when I have met him in a bar..
it would just mean so much work that so far nobody has apparently found
enough time (or considered it worth the time invested) to actually get it
finished. 

> Since another version of Nethack wouldn't know what a vacuum was, it just
> gives the character a random worthless tool. Too bad, but at least it
> doesn't break save-file compatability.

This point was just recently discussed..
I don't think random tools are a good idea here. Either unknown items
should be just ignored or there should be a replacement item somehow
defined for games that don't support the feature. Or files containing
unknown stuff should just be rejected as invalid, which is the easiest
solution and probably good enough. If the receiving game doesn't
understand every feature in the save file, it can't use it the way it was
meant to be used, anyway. And I don't think many files would be
transferred to a less capable game anyway.

